  <h2>What you can rely on</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Instrumentation never throws. A failed export, an unreachable server or a
      value that will not serialise is reported at most once every 30 seconds
      through <code>java.util.logging</code> and otherwise swallowed.
      Observability must never take down the thing it observes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Recording does not block, so your service's latency never depends on mcpd
      being fast, or up.
    </li>
    <li>
      Memory is bounded. The queue holds <code>maxQueue</code> signals; past that
      the oldest are dropped and counted, readable as
      <code>droppedSignals</code> and in <code>stats()</code>. An unbounded
      buffer turns a network outage into an out-of-memory kill.
    </li>
    <li>
      The sampling decision is made once, at the root, and inherited.
    </li>
    <li>
      A span is exported when it starts and again when it ends, so a run that
      crashed mid-flight is still visible. When a span ends before its start
      record has been sent, the record is updated in place and only one is sent.
    </li>
    <li>
      Batches are gzipped above 1 KiB and carry an idempotency key that survives
      a retry, so a request that timed out after the server stored it cannot
      double-count.
    </li>
    <li>
      Durations are measured from a monotonic clock, so an NTP step cannot
      produce a negative one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/searching" class="link">Finding a run</a> covers the filters
    in full. <a href="/docs/reference/data-model" class="link">The data model</a>
